data.content:
Yes! MSTest v3+ supports **typed `[DynamicData]`** using tuples, eliminating the `object[]` casting:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData<(string Input, bool Expected)>(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<(string Input, bool Expected)> TestData =>
[
    ("valid@email.com", true),
    ("invalid", false),
    ("", false),
];
```

**Key changes:**
- `DynamicData<(string, bool)>` — generic overload gives compile-time type safety
- Data source returns `IEnumerable<(string, bool)>` instead of `IEnumerable<object[]>`
- Tuple elements use named fields for readability

For simple constant data like this, `[DataRow]` also works and is even simpler:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DataRow("valid@email.com", true)]
[DataRow("invalid", false)]
[DataRow("", false)]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}
```

Use **typed `DynamicData<T>`** when your test data is computed or complex; use **`[DataRow]`** when values are simple constants.
